# v9.120.0: Lineage 19, DC-S06 -- the lesson the owner asked about ("Why is the story closure dependent on an external object? Is this a well-designed story?").
# The honest answer is that it was not: a design error in Lineage 18's last objective, found by the owner, recorded here as a finding.
fw:Find_ObjectiveOnExternalRegister a backlog:RetrospectiveFinding ;
    backlog:hasFindingScope backlog:Scope_Methodology ;
    backlog:hasRootCause "Lineage 18's last objective counted the structure-debt lines another session's register holds for this package, and the story that moves it (the one that shows the package clear) listed that objective among those it moves. The story's own work was finished when the package passed that session's checker; closing it still waited for the other session to delete a line, so a story's Done depended on an act its own lineage could neither perform nor prevent. Nothing in the Objective stage or the Backlog stage asks whether the package controls what a metric counts, so the design passed every rule and was still wrong." ;
    backlog:hasRemedy "An objective is measured by something this package controls: state the metric on the package's own side (for example the checker's verdict on the published package: violations new, and lines recorded for this package, as read from the package's own run), and track the other session's confirmation as a handover with its own state, never as the objective's value. A story's Done depends only on acts its own lineage can perform. Lineage 19's twelve objectives were written to this rule: each counts rows in this package's own data files or tools, and backlog_record_measures reproduces every number." .

